## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a beginner-friendly Azure data pipeline that handles schema drift, produces a dimensional model, and captures lineage. It also probes how to isolate raw data, apply transformations, and enforce simple data quality gates with observable provenance.

## Key Concepts
- Schema drift handling in Parquet
- Dimensional modeling (DimUsers, FactEvents)
- Drift capture via JSON blob
- Data quality checks (NOT NULL, timestamp parse)
- Data lineage with Purview integration

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: drift handling in a dataflow step
function transform(record) {
  const known = { user_id, event_time, event_type };
  const drift = _.omit(record, Object.keys(known));
  return { ...record, drift_json: JSON.stringify(drift) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test drift handling with evolving schemas?
- How would you extend this to handle very high throughput volumes?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes multi-tenant isolation, CDC-style ingestion from on-prem, cross-region DR, and governance in a single Azure stack. It tests practical design decisions beyond generic patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Delta Lake schema evolution and drift tolerance
- Delta Live Tables and incremental loads
- Purview lineage and tagging
- ADLS Gen2 cross-region replication
- Tenant ACLs and per-tenant partitioning

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: enforce per-tenant schema drift tolerance during ingest
function alignSchema(incoming, targetSchema){
  const allowed = targetSchema.fields.map(f=>f.name);
  const filtered = incoming.columns.filter(c => allowed.includes(c.name));
  return ensureTypes(filtered, targetSchema);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate cross-region failover and data consistency? 
- How would you backfill for tenants with late-arriving changes while preserving idempotence?
